window.WEBDEV_TOPICS = [{"title": "Your brief: a real business, a school prototype", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#challenge", "terms": " Work  in pairs  to build an informational website prototype for a real small company in Innsbruck or the surrounding area. Research its needs, contact the company, plan, design, create multimedia, program, test and present your work.  Consider a caf\u00e9, shop, service provider, sports club, guesthouse or local organization. Choose a useful audience and purpose. Both partners must participate in programming, planning, testing and reflection. You can divide tasks, but both must understand the whole website.  This is a school assignment, not professional commercial development. Public publication is optional and requires teacher approval.      Area  Your submission must include      Pages  At least four meaningful, connected HTML pages: Home, About, Products or Services, and Contact. Adapt these to your business.    HTML  Valid document structure, semantic elements, logical headings, paragraphs, navigation, working links, images with meaningful alt text, IDs and classes.    CSS  An external stylesheet; consistent colors, typography and spacing; Flexbox or Grid; readable responsive layouts on mobile and desktop.    JavaScript  An external JavaScript file and at least two meaningful interactions, such as a gallery, FAQ, filter, calculator, dark mode or mobile menu. An alert alone does not count. Explain your own code.    Multimedia  At least two suitable assets. At least one must be created or substantially edited by your pair. Document sources, permissions and edits.    GitHub  One shared repository, contributions from both partners, regular meaningful commits, organized folders "}, {"title": "Contacting a company", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#contact", "terms": "  Every pair must make at least one genuine, professional contact attempt. Receiving a reply is not compulsory.  Follow your teacher\u2019s instructions and school communication policies.   Find a suitable business and research what it does.  Use its official contact information. Personalize the message below.  Explain the school prototype and ask about useful content, audience and feedback.  If appropriate, ask for photos or a logo and explicit permission to use them.  Respect the response. Record your contact attempt without publishing private correspondence.     Email template \u00b7 personalize before sending  Copy code    Subject: School ICT project: a website prototype for [Company name]\n\nDear [Name / Company team],\n\nWe are [Names], students at [School] in Innsbruck. For our ICT project, we are learning to design and program an informational website for a local business.\n\nWe would like to create a school website prototype for [Company name]. Would you be interested in sharing a little information about your business or giving us feedback? Participation is entirely voluntary and free of charge.\n\nIf you are interested, what should a useful website tell your customers? You are welcome to suggest content and, if appropriate, provide photos or a logo that we have permission to use. Please let us know any limits on their use.\n\nThis is a learning project, not a professional website service. We cannot promise publication, hosting, a domain, ongoing support or maintenance. Any public publication would require our teacher&#x27;s approval and appropriate permissions.\n\nThank you for consid"}, {"title": "What You Must NOT Promise", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#boundaries", "terms": "  A professional commercial website or a guaranteed finished product.  Free or permanent hosting, a custom domain, maintenance or ongoing technical support.  Search-engine rankings, online payments, real booking systems or user accounts.  Features you cannot implement or an official website without authorization.    You are creating a school website prototype.  The company may choose to use it, but you are not promising to develop, host or maintain an official website. With teacher approval, you may provide source files. The company must arrange hosting, a domain, maintenance and legal compliance for official use. Do not promise to operate its website through your personal GitHub account.   Ask permission for logos and copyrighted materials unless a suitable licence already permits use. Keep credits and references.  Use original or properly licensed photos. Obtain appropriate permission for identifiable people.  Do not share confidential information, publish private contact details or collect visitors\u2019 personal information unnecessarily.  Clearly label unofficial prototypes and obtain teacher approval before any public publication.  "}, {"title": "Your project, one milestone at a time", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#phases", "terms": " There is no fixed timeline. Your teacher determines due dates and presentations. These phases are a guide, not a strict sequence: return to earlier work whenever needed. Progress, learning and problem-solving matter more than speed.   0 tasks complete    Saved in this browser only. Progress is not synchronized across devices or submitted to your teacher.  Reset progress     0 / 5 01 \u00b7 Find and Contact a Company     Research suitable businesses.   Select a company.   Contact the company.   Create a shared GitHub repository.   Document the initial idea.   Milestone:  Company selected, contact attempted and repository created.     0 / 7 02 \u00b7 Plan Your Website     Identify the intended audience.   Decide what content the website needs.   Create a sitemap.   Sketch page layouts.   Choose colors and typography.   Plan multimedia.   Divide responsibilities.   Milestone:  A clear plan and design concept.     0 / 5 03 \u00b7 Build the HTML Structure     Create the HTML pages.   Build navigation.   Add meaningful content.   Use semantic HTML.   Organize files and folders.   Milestone:  A functioning multi-page structure.     0 / 5 04 \u00b7 Design with CSS     Apply consistent styling.   Use Flexbox or Grid.   Improve spacing and typography.   Make the website responsive.   Test different screen sizes.   Milestone:  A readable, professionally styled website.     0 / 4 05 \u00b7 Add JavaScript     Implement two meaningful interactions.   Use events and functions.   Manipulate HTML elements.   Test and debug the features.   Milestone:  Two functioning and understood JavaScript interactions.     0 / "}, {"title": "Using AI: Your Personal Programming Tutor", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#ai", "terms": " Artificial Intelligence is becoming an important part of computer science and professional software development.  For this reason, you are allowed to use ChatGPT during this project.  However, AI is only permitted as a learning assistant.  The goal is for YOU to learn how to develop a website, not for AI to create your website.  You may use ChatGPT to understand concepts, receive hints, debug code, ask questions and improve your understanding.  You must not ask ChatGPT to complete the project for you.  At the end of the project, you must be able to understand and explain the code you submit.   AI is your tutor, not your developer.  This is a compulsory part of the assignment.   Create or sign in to a ChatGPT account only if school policy and applicable age requirements permit it. Otherwise your teacher will arrange an approved alternative.  Start one new conversation for this project. Copy the complete prompt below and paste it as the first message.  Continue in that conversation for continuity. Ask for explanations, hints, debugging guidance and feedback.  Write your actual project code yourself. Test suggestions, protect private information and be ready to explain your AI use.   The one-conversation rule is a classroom workflow, not a technical guarantee. A prompt cannot enforce the rules for you. You remain responsible for your work. You do not have to publicly share your private conversation history.    OFFICIAL SOCRATIC TUTOR PROMPT  Copy Tutor Prompt    You are now my personal programming tutor for a school project.\n\nI am a secondary-school student learning HTML, CSS"}, {"title": "Make multimedia meaningful", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#multimedia", "terms": " Create or substantially edit at least one of your two required assets: an original photograph, edited product image, illustration, poster or short video. Use visuals to communicate, not simply fill space. For an extension, create an edited promotional video, advertising graphic or coherent photo series.      Choice  Use it well      JPEG / WebP  Good for photographs. Export at a quality that keeps detail without excessive file size. Check browser support for your intended audience.    PNG  Useful for transparency and sharp screenshots; photos can produce large files.    SVG  Scalable vector graphics for original icons and diagrams. Only use trusted SVG files.    Resolution  Match pixel dimensions to the largest expected display size. A small source becomes blurry when enlarged.    Optimization  Crop, resize, compare the export and record file sizes. Use width and height attributes to reduce layout shifts.    Attribution  Record creator, source URL, licence, permission and your edits in a credits file or README. Publicly visible does not automatically mean free to reuse.       Try it yourself  Make two exports of the same image. Compare their file sizes and visual quality, then justify your choice.  "}, {"title": "Feedback is part of the work", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#assessment", "terms": " Assessment is predominantly formative: progress, understanding, problem-solving and individual contribution matter alongside the final website. These descriptors support feedback; the teacher determines numerical weighting separately.      Criterion  Developing  Secure  Extending      HTML & content  Some pages or links need work.  Four clear pages, accurate content and meaningful HTML.  Structure is accessible, purposeful and carefully checked.    CSS & responsiveness  Layout or readability needs improvement.  Consistent design works at different widths.  Design decisions serve the audience; edge cases are refined.    JavaScript  Features are incomplete or difficult to explain.  Two useful features work and can be explained.  Features are robust, accessible and handle invalid input.    GitHub & teamwork  Contributions or commits are uneven.  Both partners contribute and keep useful commits.  Pair coordinates changes well and resolves problems together.    Planning & development  Evidence of planning and testing is limited.  Research, contact attempt, multimedia and tests are documented.  Feedback leads to thoughtful, documented improvements.    Presentation & understanding  Explanations rely on reading unfamiliar code.  Each partner explains code, decisions, debugging and AI use.  Each partner evaluates trade-offs and proposes improvements.      Pause and check your understanding   Can I explain how my HTML pages connect and how CSS selectors work?  Can I explain each JavaScript event listener and function?  Have I tested different screen sizes and keyboard use?  Can both"}, {"title": "Something Doesn\u2019t Work? Start Here.", "page": "Build something real.", "url": "index.html#debug", "terms": "  Describe what should happen.  Observe what actually happens.  Open browser developer tools (usually F12 or right-click \u2192 Inspect). Look at the Console.  Find the smallest relevant part of the code. Read the filename and line number in the error.  Make one change. Use console.log() to inspect a value.  Reload, test the same action and check the result.       Problem  First checks      HTML: links or images fail  Compare filenames and capitalization exactly. Check relative paths. Make sure the target file exists. Inspect nesting and closing tags.    CSS: styles missing  Check the stylesheet link in the head, the file path and selector spelling. Use the Styles panel to see which rule wins.    CSS: awkward layout  Inspect the box model. Margin is outside the border; padding is inside. Avoid fixed widths that cause overflow. Check position and the containing block.    JS: nothing happens  Check the script reference and defer. Match IDs and function names exactly. Confirm the listener is attached to the intended element.    SyntaxError  Check quotes, brackets and parentheses at and before the reported line.    ReferenceError / undefined  Check spelling, declaration and scope. Log the value before using it.    Cannot read properties of null  Your selector found no element. Check the ID and whether the script ran before HTML was parsed.        script.js  Copy code    console.log(&#x27;script loaded&#x27;);\nconst button = document.getElementById(&#x27;menu-button&#x27;);\nconsole.log(button); // null means no matching element was found   "}, {"title": "The document: head and body", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#structure", "terms": " HTML describes content and its structure. Elements tell the browser what each part means. Save this complete reference as index.html.    Code  Copy code    &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Alpine Coffee | Home&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;style.css&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;script src=&quot;script.js&quot; defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Alpine Coffee&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;&lt;p&gt;A fictional caf\u00e9 in Innsbruck.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;School learning example.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;    The doctype selects standards mode; it is not an HTML element.  html  is the root, with a language for assistive tools.  head  contains metadata, the browser-tab title and file links.  body  contains visible page content. UTF-8 supports international characters. The viewport lets responsive layouts use device width.  defer  runs an external script after parsing.   Try it yourself  Change the browser-tab title and visible heading. Explain why they are separate.  "}, {"title": "Elements, attributes and nesting", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#elements", "terms": " Most elements have an opening tag, content and a closing tag. Attributes supply extra information inside the opening tag.    Code  Copy code    &lt;p&gt;Try our &lt;strong&gt;seasonal coffee&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;menu.html&quot; class=&quot;button&quot;&gt;Read the menu&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;images/coffee.svg&quot; alt=&quot;A cup of coffee&quot;&gt;    The complete  &lt;a&gt;\u2026&lt;/a&gt;  is an element.  href  is an attribute and  menu.html  its value. Close the inner element before the outer one. Images are void elements: they have no closing tag.   Try it yourself  Add emphasis to one phrase without making the entire paragraph bold.  "}, {"title": "Headings, paragraphs and text", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#text", "terms": " Use headings to communicate hierarchy. Start with a clear page heading, then use h2 for major sections and h3 for subsections.    Code  Copy code    &lt;h1&gt;Our caf\u00e9&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;Our story&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;We started with a love of &lt;strong&gt;good coffee&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Try our &lt;em&gt;seasonal&lt;/em&gt; special.&lt;br&gt;Served warm.&lt;/p&gt;    Use  strong  for importance and  em  for emphasis. Use CSS for visual styling rather than choosing headings by size. Paragraphs create text blocks; br is a line break, not a spacing tool.   Rendered result  Our story  We started with a love of  good coffee .  "}, {"title": "Links, navigation and multiple pages", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#links", "terms": " Each HTML file is a separate page. Links connect the pages. index.html is the conventional entry page when a web server opens a folder.    Code  Copy code    &lt;nav aria-label=&quot;Main navigation&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;index.html&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;about.html&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;menu.html&quot;&gt;Menu&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;contact.html&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;#opening-hours&quot;&gt;Jump to opening hours&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;section id=&quot;opening-hours&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Opening hours&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;/section&gt;    The  href  gives a destination. A hash links to an ID on the page;  contact.html#opening-hours  links to a section on another page. Use descriptive link text. Use links to navigate and buttons to perform actions.   Try it yourself  Add a fifth navigation link and create its matching HTML file.  "}, {"title": "Images and alternative text", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#images", "terms": " An image needs a correct path and an appropriate text alternative. Describe its purpose in context.    Code  Copy code    &lt;img src=&quot;images/coffee.svg&quot;\n     alt=&quot;Illustration of a steaming cup of coffee&quot;\n     width=&quot;400&quot; height=&quot;300&quot;&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;images/decorative-line.svg&quot; alt=&quot;&quot;&gt;    Use empty  alt=\"\"  for purely decorative images. Do not omit alt or use a filename as a description. Width and height reserve space. Use CSS  max-width: 100%; height: auto;  for responsive images. The second line illustrates a decorative asset; create that file before using it.   Rendered result     Try it yourself  Write useful alternative text for one of your business photos.  "}, {"title": "Lists, divs and spans", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#lists", "terms": " Use lists for collections. Use div or span only when a more meaningful element does not fit.    Code  Copy code    &lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Espresso&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Tea&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;Choose a drink.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Visit the counter.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;\n&lt;div class=&quot;product-card&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Espresso &lt;span class=&quot;price&quot;&gt;\u20ac3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;     ul  is unordered;  ol  has an order. Each item is an li. A div is a generic block container; span is a generic inline container. Neither explains meaning by itself. "}, {"title": "Semantic page structure", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#semantic", "terms": " Semantic elements describe the role of content and make your document easier to navigate and maintain.    Code  Copy code    &lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Alpine Coffee&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;\n&lt;nav aria-label=&quot;Main&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;index.html&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;main&gt;\n  &lt;section aria-labelledby=&quot;specials-title&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;h2 id=&quot;specials-title&quot;&gt;Our specials&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;article&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Seasonal blend&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;A rich roast.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/article&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/main&gt;\n&lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Fictional school example.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;    Use one main landmark for the page\u2019s main content. Header introduces, nav groups navigation, section groups a topic, article is self-contained content, and footer closes a page or section.   Try it yourself  Sketch your four pages using semantic containers before styling them.  "}, {"title": "IDs, classes and buttons", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#ids", "terms": " An ID uniquely identifies one element in a document. A class groups elements that share a style or behavior.    Code  Copy code    &lt;h2 id=&quot;welcome&quot; class=&quot;section-title&quot;&gt;Welcome&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;h2 class=&quot;section-title&quot;&gt;Our menu&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;welcome-button&quot;&gt;Say hello&lt;/button&gt;    Do not repeat an ID on the same page. Classes may be reused, and one element may have several classes. The button needs JavaScript to change the heading: see  getElementById and events . See also  CSS selectors .   Try it yourself  Add a shared class to two elements and give only one a unique ID.  "}, {"title": "Tables for structured data", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#tables", "terms": " Use tables for information with row and column relationships, not for page layout.    Code  Copy code    &lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;caption&gt;Illustrative opening hours&lt;/caption&gt;\n  &lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Day&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Hours&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;\n  &lt;tbody&gt;\n    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th scope=&quot;row&quot;&gt;Monday&lt;/th&gt;&lt;td&gt;08:00\u201317:00&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th scope=&quot;row&quot;&gt;Sunday&lt;/th&gt;&lt;td&gt;Closed&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;/tbody&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;    Caption names the table; th identifies headers; scope states whether a header describes a row or column. td contains ordinary data. "}, {"title": "Forms, inputs and labels", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#forms", "terms": " A label explains an input. Match its for attribute to the input\u2019s ID. Browser validation helps users enter suitable values.    Code  Copy code    &lt;form id=&quot;demo-form&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;guest-email&quot;&gt;Email (demonstration only)&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input id=&quot;guest-email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; required&gt;\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Check format&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;p id=&quot;feedback&quot; role=&quot;status&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;    Use suitable types such as text, email or number; select offers choices and textarea accepts longer text. A placeholder is not a replacement for a label.  A form does not send email or store data by itself.  Add the demonstration handler in  form validation . Do not collect real visitor information in your prototype.   Try it yourself  Create a labelled quantity input with minimum 1 and maximum 10.  "}, {"title": "File paths and external files", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#paths", "terms": " A relative path starts from the file that contains it. Keep names short, lowercase and consistent.    Code  Copy code    &lt;!-- In index.html at the website root --&gt;\n&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;style.css&quot;&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;script.js&quot; defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;images/coffee.svg&quot; alt=&quot;Coffee illustration&quot;&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;about.html&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;!-- In a page one folder deeper --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;../index.html&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;     ../  goes up one folder. HTML paths resolve relative to the HTML page; CSS url() paths resolve relative to the stylesheet. Avoid paths starting with  /  for this portable project. Filenames can be case-sensitive when hosted. Keep index.html at the publication root.   Try it yourself  Draw the path from a page in a subfolder to an image in your root images folder.  "}, {"title": "Common HTML mistakes", "page": "HTML: give content structure.", "url": "html.html#mistakes", "terms": "     Symptom  What to inspect      Text unexpectedly bold  Look for a missing closing strong tag.    Missing image  Check src, extension, capitalization and whether the file is present.    Broken navigation  Check href and the exact name of the other HTML file.    Unexpected page structure  Inspect invalid nesting; do not put a div inside a p.    Controls hard to use  Add labels, use real buttons and provide meaningful link text.      Use the browser Elements panel to compare the parsed document with your source. Browsers repair some invalid markup, which can hide your mistake. "}, {"title": "One stylesheet, every page", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#syntax", "terms": " CSS describes presentation. Link the same stylesheet in every page\u2019s head so shared design stays consistent.    Code  Copy code    /* style.css */\n:root { --accent: #315bdb; }\nbody { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; color: #1c2636; }\nh1 { color: var(--accent); }\n.card { background: white; padding: 1.5rem; }    A rule has a selector and a block of property: value declarations. End each declaration with a semicolon. In HTML use  &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"&gt; . External stylesheets avoid repeating style rules in every page. "}, {"title": "Selectors: elements, classes and IDs", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#selectors", "terms": " Select all elements of a type, reusable groups, or one unique element.    Code  Copy code    p { line-height: 1.6; }\n.product { border: 1px solid #ccd5e0; }\n#special-offer { background: #edf2ff; }\n.product a { color: #315bdb; }     p  selects paragraphs,  .product  selects a class, and  #special-offer  selects an ID. The last rule selects links inside a product. In ordinary rules, specificity and source order help decide which declaration wins. Avoid unnecessary ID styling when reusable classes will do.   Try it yourself  Style two cards using the same class.  "}, {"title": "Typography, colors and units", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#type", "terms": " Choose readable text and sufficient contrast. Use a few consistent sizes.    Code  Copy code    body { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; }\nh1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem); }\n.intro { max-width: 65ch; }\n.banner { color: #ffffff; background-color: #254cbb; }\n.logo { width: 80%; max-width: 20rem; }    rem relates to the root font size, em often relates to an element\u2019s font size, px is a CSS pixel, % depends on the property and containing context, vw is a percentage of viewport width, and ch roughly measures the width of the zero glyph. clamp sets a minimum, flexible preferred value and maximum. "}, {"title": "Color playground", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#colors", "terms": " Compare foreground and background colors. The ratio is calculated from relative luminance. Aim for at least 4.5:1 for ordinary text; 3:1 applies to large text. Color alone should not communicate meaning.    Text color   Background    Good coffee. Clear communication.       Live CSS \u00b7 color playground  Copy code       "}, {"title": "The box model", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#box-model", "terms": " Every box has content, padding, border and margin. Padding creates space inside the border; margin separates a box from nearby elements.    Code  Copy code    * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n.card {\n  width: 100%;\n  max-width: 30rem;\n  min-height: 8rem;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border: 5px solid #507bc1;\n  margin: 20px;\n}    With border-box, the stated width includes padding and border, but not margin. Fixed heights can clip content, so use natural height or min-height where possible. "}, {"title": "Box model playground", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#box", "terms": "   Margin  20 px   Padding  20 px   Border  5 px     Content Blue inside \u00b7 gold outside      Live CSS \u00b7 box model  Copy code         Try it yourself  Set padding to zero, then margin to zero. Explain which space disappears each time.  "}, {"title": "Display, sizing and positioning", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#display", "terms": " Block boxes usually start on a new line. Inline content flows with text. Flex and grid create layout systems.    Code  Copy code    .hidden { display: none; }\n.badge { display: inline-block; padding: 0.3rem; }\n.card { position: relative; }\n.card .label { position: absolute; top: 1rem; right: 1rem; }\n.sidebar { position: sticky; top: 1rem; }    display: none removes an element from layout and the accessibility tree. relative keeps normal-flow space; absolute positions against its containing block, often the nearest positioned ancestor. fixed is usually viewport-relative. sticky follows scrolling within its scrolling context and containing block. Use normal layout before reaching for positioning. "}, {"title": "Flexbox playground", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#flexbox", "terms": " Flexbox arranges items along one main axis. justify-content works along that axis; align-items works across it. Changing direction changes the axes.    Direction  row  column    Justify content  flex-start  center  space-between  space-around  flex-end    Align items  stretch  center  flex-start  flex-end    Gap     01  02 Menu  03      Live CSS \u00b7 flexbox  Copy code         Try it yourself  Center all three cards horizontally and vertically, then change to column. Predict what will move.  "}, {"title": "CSS Grid: rows and columns", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#grid", "terms": " Grid is useful for page regions and two-dimensional collections such as product cards.    Code  Copy code    .products {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  .products { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\n}    1fr shares remaining space. minmax(0, 1fr) allows tracks to shrink below their content\u2019s minimum width. gap separates cells. At 600px or narrower, this layout becomes one column.   Try it yourself  Change three columns to two. Then try repeat(auto-fit, minmax(12rem, 1fr)).  "}, {"title": "Responsive design and media queries", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#responsive", "terms": " Let content fit its available space. Avoid assuming everyone uses the same screen or input device.    Code  Copy code    img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n.container { width: min(100% - 2rem, 70rem); margin: auto; }\n.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\n}    The media query responds to viewport width, not the type or brand of device. Keep the viewport meta tag in the HTML head. Test long text, zoom, portrait orientation and keyboard navigation. "}, {"title": "Resize a real viewport", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#responsive-demo", "terms": " This embedded page has its own viewport. Slide below 600px and watch the media query switch to one column. On a narrow screen, the preview is capped to the available space.   Preview width:  700 px        CSS inside the preview  Copy code    /* The full source is in examples/responsive.html */\n.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 12px; }\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\n}   "}, {"title": "Button designer", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#button", "terms": " Change the values and then hover or focus the button. Hover styling should have a keyboard focus equivalent.    Padding   Corner radius   Background   Text   Hover / focus     Explore our menu        Live CSS \u00b7 button  Copy code         Try it yourself  Create a button that matches your business identity. Check its text contrast using the color playground.  "}, {"title": "Variables, reusable styles and transitions", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#variables", "terms": " Custom properties keep repeated values consistent. Reuse a class for components that share a purpose.    Code  Copy code    :root { --brand: #315bdb; --space: 1rem; }\n.button {\n  background: var(--brand);\n  padding: var(--space);\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  transition: background-color 150ms ease;\n}\n.button:hover, .button:focus-visible { background: #21409c; }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .button { transition: none; }\n}    Variables participate in the cascade. Transitions animate a change between property values; they do not create the change. Avoid animating everything, and respect reduced-motion preferences. "}, {"title": "When CSS does not behave", "page": "CSS: make it feel right.", "url": "css.html#problems", "terms": "     Check  Why      Stylesheet path  A wrong href prevents the file from loading.    Selector and class names  The HTML class must match your .class selector.    Styles panel  Crossed-out declarations can reveal competing rules.    Margin and padding  They affect different parts of the box model.    Overflow  Look for wide fixed sizes, long unbroken text or grid tracks that cannot shrink.    Positioning  Check the containing block before changing top or left.     "}, {"title": "Three languages, one website", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#start", "terms": "  HTML creates the content. CSS controls the appearance. JavaScript makes the website interactive.     01  Find an element  Select the button already in the HTML document.    02  Listen for an event  Wait for the visitor to click it.    03  Run instructions  Call a function that changes the page.   "}, {"title": "Load script.js and understand defer", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#connect", "terms": " The browser reads your HTML and creates a document model. An external script can then find and change those elements.    Code  Copy code    &lt;!-- In the head of each HTML file --&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;script.js&quot; defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;    src names the JavaScript file. defer lets the browser download a classic external script while parsing HTML and execute it after parsing finishes, before DOMContentLoaded. Without it, a script in the head may look for elements that do not yet exist. Do not put HTML tags inside script.js. "}, {"title": "Variables, values and types", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#variables", "terms": " Variables give names to values. const prevents reassignment of that binding; let allows reassignment.    Code  Copy code    const caf\u00e9Name = &quot;Alpine Coffee&quot;; // string\nconst price = 3.5;               // number\nlet quantity = 2;\nconst available = true;          // boolean\nquantity = 3;\nconst total = price * quantity;\nconsole.log(caf\u00e9Name, total, available);    Strings use quotes. Numbers support arithmetic. Booleans are true or false. const does not make the contents of an object or array immutable. Prefer clear names. Input.value is a string, even for a number input; convert it before arithmetic.   Try it yourself  Predict total, then change quantity and check with console.log().  "}, {"title": "Functions and parameters", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#functions", "terms": " A function groups instructions you can run again. Parameters are names for values supplied to a call.    Code  Copy code    function calculateTotal(price, quantity) {\n  return price * quantity;\n}\nconst total = calculateTotal(3.5, 2);\nconsole.log(total); // 7    price and quantity are parameters. 3.5 and 2 are arguments in this call. return supplies the result to the caller. Defining a function does not call it.   Try it yourself  Add a third parameter for a fixed discount. Predict a result before testing.  "}, {"title": "Conditions and comparisons", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#conditions", "terms": " Use a condition to choose what to do. A comparison produces a boolean.    Code  Copy code    const quantity = 3;\nif (quantity &gt;= 5) {\n  console.log(&quot;Group order&quot;);\n} else {\n  console.log(&quot;Small order&quot;);\n}\nconsole.log(quantity === 3); // true\nconsole.log(quantity !== 3); // false    Use === for strict equality and !== for inequality. &gt;, &lt;, &gt;= and &lt;= compare values. = assigns a value. &amp;&amp; means both conditions, || means at least one, and ! negates. Avoid confusing assignment with comparison. "}, {"title": "Arrays and loops", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#arrays", "terms": " An array keeps a list of values. Loops repeat work for each item.    Code  Copy code    const drinks = [&quot;Espresso&quot;, &quot;Tea&quot;, &quot;Cocoa&quot;];\nconsole.log(drinks[0]); // Espresso: indexes start at 0\nfor (const drink of drinks) {\n  console.log(drink);\n}\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; drinks.length; i++) {\n  console.log(i, drinks[i]);\n}    length is the number of items. The first loop visits each value. The second uses an index, continuing while the condition is true. i++ increases i by one.   Try it yourself  Add a fourth drink, then explain why the loops still work.  "}, {"title": "The DOM and selectors", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#dom", "terms": " The Document Object Model represents your page as nodes and elements. JavaScript can select elements using IDs or CSS selectors.    Code  Copy code    const heading = document.getElementById(&quot;welcome&quot;);\nconst firstCard = document.querySelector(&quot;.product&quot;);\nconst allCards = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.product&quot;);\nheading.textContent = &quot;Welcome to our caf\u00e9&quot;;\nfor (const card of allCards) {\n  card.classList.add(&quot;featured&quot;);\n}    getElementById takes the ID without #. querySelector returns the first match; querySelectorAll returns a collection. A single selector with no match returns null. textContent changes plain text.  Avoid inserting untrusted content with innerHTML:  it parses markup and can introduce security problems. Prefer textContent when changing text. Related:  HTML IDs and classes . "}, {"title": "Events: onclick and addEventListener", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#events", "terms": " An event is something that happens, such as click, input or submit. Register a function to respond.    Code  Copy code    const button = document.getElementById(&quot;welcome-button&quot;);\nfunction greet() {\n  console.log(&quot;Hello!&quot;);\n}\nbutton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, greet);\n// Alternative: button.onclick = greet;\n// Use one approach here, not both. Inline onclick attributes exist,\n// but keeping handlers in script.js separates structure and behavior.    Pass greet, not greet(), so the function runs on the event rather than immediately. onclick stores one handler; addEventListener can register multiple independent listeners. A real button also supports keyboard activation. "}, {"title": "01 \u00b7 Alert button", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#alert", "terms": " Objective: attach a click listener and open a simple message. This introduces events, but an alert alone does not meet the project\u2019s feature requirement.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;hello&quot;&gt;Say hello&lt;/button&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const button = document.getElementById(&quot;hello&quot;);\nbutton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  alert(&quot;Welcome to Alpine Coffee!&quot;);\n});     The listener waits for click. The function is then called, and alert opens a browser dialog. Dismiss it to continue.   Open standalone example \u2197  \u00b7 Each example loads  style.css  and  script.js  with  defer . Shared base styles make controls readable.   Try it yourself  Change the message. Explain when the function runs.  "}, {"title": "02 \u00b7 Change text", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#change-text", "terms": " Objective: connect a button to a heading by their unique HTML IDs.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;h2 id=&quot;welcome&quot;&gt;Hello, visitor.&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;welcome-button&quot;&gt;Welcome me&lt;/button&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const button = document.getElementById(&quot;welcome-button&quot;);\nconst heading = document.getElementById(&quot;welcome&quot;);\nbutton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  heading.textContent = &quot;Welcome to Alpine Coffee!&quot;;\n});     HTML creates the two elements. getElementById retrieves them. addEventListener waits. textContent replaces the heading text after the click. No page reload is needed.   Open standalone example \u2197  \u00b7 Each example loads  style.css  and  script.js  with  defer . Shared base styles make controls readable.   Try it yourself  Change the greeting, then add a second button that restores it.  "}, {"title": "03 \u00b7 Toggle a design class", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#dark-mode", "terms": " Objective: use a CSS class to change a whole region\u2019s appearance.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;theme&quot; aria-pressed=&quot;false&quot;&gt;Dark mode&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;The same content, a different theme.&lt;/p&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const button = document.getElementById(&quot;theme&quot;);\nbutton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  const isDark = document.body.classList.toggle(&quot;dark&quot;);\n  button.setAttribute(&quot;aria-pressed&quot;, String(isDark));\n});       style.css \u00b7 feature styles  Copy code    body.dark { background: #172233; color: #f4f7fc; }      classList.toggle adds a class if absent and removes it if present. The CSS does the visual work. aria-pressed tells assistive technology whether the toggle is active.   Open standalone example \u2197  \u00b7 Each example loads  style.css  and  script.js  with  defer . Shared base styles make controls readable.   Try it yourself  Create a second color theme and describe why CSS belongs in the stylesheet.  "}, {"title": "04 \u00b7 Image gallery", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#gallery", "terms": " Objective: change an image source and its alternative text together.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;img id=&quot;photo&quot; src=&quot;../../images/coffee.svg&quot; alt=&quot;A steaming coffee cup&quot; width=&quot;200&quot; height=&quot;150&quot;&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;&lt;button id=&quot;next&quot; type=&quot;button&quot;&gt;Next image&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;p id=&quot;caption&quot; aria-live=&quot;polite&quot;&gt;Coffee \u00b7 1 of 3&lt;/p&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const images = [\n  {src: &quot;../../images/coffee.svg&quot;, alt: &quot;A steaming coffee cup&quot;, name: &quot;Coffee&quot;},\n  {src: &quot;../../images/mountains.svg&quot;, alt: &quot;Layers of Alpine peaks&quot;, name: &quot;Mountains&quot;},\n  {src: &quot;../../images/pastry.svg&quot;, alt: &quot;A golden croissant&quot;, name: &quot;Pastry&quot;}\n];\nlet index = 0;\nconst photo = document.getElementById(&quot;photo&quot;);\nconst caption = document.getElementById(&quot;caption&quot;);\ndocument.getElementById(&quot;next&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  index = (index + 1) % images.length;\n  photo.src = images[index].src;\n  photo.alt = images[index].alt;\n  caption.textContent = images[index].name + &quot; \u00b7 &quot; + (index + 1) + &quot; of 3&quot;;\n});       style.css \u00b7 feature styles  Copy code    img { display: block; object-fit: cover; margin-bottom: 10px; }      The array stores objects with matching paths, descriptions and captions. % wraps the index back to zero. Updating alt ensures the new image still has a correct description.   Open standalone example "}, {"title": "05 \u00b7 FAQ accordion", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#faq", "terms": " Objective: show and hide an answer while keeping its state accessible.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;question&quot; aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot; aria-controls=&quot;answer&quot;&gt;Do you offer plant-based milk?&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;p id=&quot;answer&quot; hidden&gt;Yes. Ask about our oat drink option.&lt;/p&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const question = document.getElementById(&quot;question&quot;);\nconst answer = document.getElementById(&quot;answer&quot;);\nquestion.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  answer.hidden = !answer.hidden;\n  question.setAttribute(&quot;aria-expanded&quot;, String(!answer.hidden));\n});     hidden controls visibility. ! reverses a boolean. aria-controls links the button to the answer; aria-expanded reports its state. Native details/summary is another useful option when custom JavaScript is unnecessary.   Open standalone example \u2197  \u00b7 Each example loads  style.css  and  script.js  with  defer . Shared base styles make controls readable.   Try it yourself  Add a second independent question and answer.  "}, {"title": "06 \u00b7 Product filter", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#filter", "terms": " Objective: compare a selected category with each product\u2019s data attribute.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;label for=&quot;category&quot;&gt;Show products&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;select id=&quot;category&quot;&gt;&lt;option value=&quot;all&quot;&gt;All&lt;/option&gt;&lt;option value=&quot;drink&quot;&gt;Drinks&lt;/option&gt;&lt;option value=&quot;food&quot;&gt;Food&lt;/option&gt;&lt;/select&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;product&quot; data-category=&quot;drink&quot;&gt;Espresso&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li class=&quot;product&quot; data-category=&quot;drink&quot;&gt;Tea&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li class=&quot;product&quot; data-category=&quot;food&quot;&gt;Croissant&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;p id=&quot;count&quot; role=&quot;status&quot;&gt;3 products shown&lt;/p&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const category = document.getElementById(&quot;category&quot;);\nconst products = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.product&quot;);\ncategory.addEventListener(&quot;change&quot;, function () {\n  let count = 0;\n  for (const product of products) {\n    product.hidden = category.value !== &quot;all&quot; &amp;&amp;\n      product.dataset.category !== category.value;\n    if (!product.hidden) count++;\n  }\n  document.getElementById(&quot;count&quot;).textContent = count + &quot; products shown&quot;;\n});     change fires when the choice changes. dataset.category reads data-category. A product is hidden only when the selection is not all and its category does not match.   Open standalone example \u2197  \u00b7 Each example loads  style.css  and  script.js  with  defer . Shared base styles make controls readable"}, {"title": "07 \u00b7 Price calculator", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#calculator", "terms": " Objective: read and validate input before calculating a total.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;label for=&quot;quantity&quot;&gt;Coffee quantity (\u20ac3.50 each)&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input type=&quot;number&quot; id=&quot;quantity&quot; min=&quot;1&quot; max=&quot;20&quot; step=&quot;1&quot; value=&quot;1&quot;&gt;\n&lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;calculate&quot;&gt;Calculate&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;p id=&quot;total&quot; role=&quot;status&quot;&gt;Choose a quantity.&lt;/p&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const quantity = document.getElementById(&quot;quantity&quot;);\nconst total = document.getElementById(&quot;total&quot;);\ndocument.getElementById(&quot;calculate&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  const number = Number(quantity.value);\n  if (!Number.isInteger(number) || number &lt; 1 || number &gt; 20) {\n    total.textContent = &quot;Enter a whole number from 1 to 20.&quot;;\n    return;\n  }\n  total.textContent = &quot;Total: \u20ac&quot; + (number * 3.5).toFixed(2);\n});       style.css \u00b7 feature styles  Copy code    input { width: 70px; margin: 8px; }      Number converts the string from value. Number.isInteger and range checks reject unsuitable values. return ends the handler early for invalid input. toFixed(2) formats two decimal places. This is a demonstration, not a payment system.   Open standalone example \u2197  \u00b7 Each example loads  style.css  and  script.js  with  defer . Shared base styles make controls readable.   Try it yourself  Add a second product with a different price. Decide what inputs you need.  "}, {"title": "08 \u00b7 Responsive navigation", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#mobile-nav", "terms": " Objective: toggle a compact menu and keep it visible on larger viewports. Resize the standalone example to test both layouts.       index.html \u00b7 inside body  Copy code    &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; id=&quot;menu-button&quot; aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot; aria-controls=&quot;links&quot;&gt;Menu&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;nav id=&quot;links&quot; aria-label=&quot;Demo navigation&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;#home&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#about&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;h2 id=&quot;home&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p id=&quot;about&quot;&gt;A fictional caf\u00e9 demo.&lt;/p&gt;      script.js  Copy code    const button = document.getElementById(&quot;menu-button&quot;);\nconst links = document.getElementById(&quot;links&quot;);\nbutton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  const open = links.classList.toggle(&quot;open&quot;);\n  button.setAttribute(&quot;aria-expanded&quot;, String(open));\n});\ndocument.addEventListener(&quot;keydown&quot;, function (event) {\n  if (event.key === &quot;Escape&quot;) {\n    links.classList.remove(&quot;open&quot;);\n    button.setAttribute(&quot;aria-expanded&quot;, &quot;false&quot;);\n    button.focus();\n  }\n});       style.css \u00b7 feature styles  Copy code    nav { display: flex; gap: 16px; }\n#menu-button { display: none; }\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  #menu-button { display: inline-block; }\n  nav { display: none; }\n  nav.open { display: flex; }\n}      CSS handles the breakpoint; JavaScript toggles the open class. The button exposes expanded state and Escape closes the compact menu. Links remain normal navi"}, {"title": "Basic form validation", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#validation", "terms": " Use built-in constraints and prevent submission in a local demonstration. Validation in a browser helps usability; it is not a security boundary for a real server.    Code  Copy code    // Use with the form shown on the HTML reference page.\nconst form = document.getElementById(&quot;demo-form&quot;);\nform.addEventListener(&quot;submit&quot;, function (event) {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  document.getElementById(&quot;feedback&quot;).textContent =\n    &quot;Format accepted. Nothing was sent or saved.&quot;;\n});    The browser checks required and type=email before a normal submit event. preventDefault stops navigation/submission. A real service would require an approved backend and server-side validation; this project does not require one.   Try it yourself  Test an empty field and a malformed address using invented data.  "}, {"title": "Read the error, test one change", "page": "JavaScript: bring it to life.", "url": "javascript.html#debugging", "terms": " Open developer tools \u2192 Console. A red error usually names a file and line. Check the first relevant error before adding more code.      Error  Likely cause      SyntaxError  Missing quote, bracket or parenthesis.    ReferenceError  A variable or function name does not exist in this scope.    TypeError involving null  A selector did not find the intended element.    No visible error  Log inside the listener. Verify the event and input value.        script.js  Copy code    console.log(&quot;The script loaded&quot;);\nconsole.log(document.getElementById(&quot;welcome-button&quot;));    Use breakpoints in the Sources/Debugger panel to pause before a statement and inspect values. See the  step-by-step debugging guide . "}, {"title": "Git keeps history. GitHub helps you share it.", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#concepts", "terms": "     Term  Meaning      Git  The version-control system that records changes to files.    GitHub  An online service that hosts Git repositories and collaboration tools.    Repository  Your project files plus their recorded history.    Commit  A recorded snapshot of selected changes with a message.    Clone  Make a local copy of a repository and its history.    Push  Send your local commits to the remote repository.    Pull  Bring remote changes into your local branch, integrating them.    Branch  A named line of development, useful for separate work.    Merge  Combine changes from different lines of development.    Collaborator  Someone granted permission to contribute to a repository.    GitHub Pages  GitHub\u2019s hosting service for static websites.    Hosting  A service that makes site files available to visitors online.    Domain name  A readable address such as example.org. It is separate from your source files and hosting.       Edit locally  \u2192  Commit  \u2192  Push to GitHub  Before the next edit: fetch and pull your partner\u2019s changes.  "}, {"title": "Create your shared repository", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#repository", "terms": "  Sign in with your own school-approved GitHub account. Do not share a password.  Use the + menu \u2192 New repository. Choose a clear name such as alpine-coffee-prototype.  Use the teacher-approved visibility. Prefer a private repository while developing; do not publish confidential or personal information. Add a README and create the repository.  Open Add file \u2192 Upload files. Upload your HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images with their folder structure intact.  Write a meaningful message such as \u201cAdd initial four-page structure\u201d and commit the changes. Check that the files appear in the repository.   A browser upload records a commit directly on GitHub. Editing a local clone uses a local commit followed by a push. These are two workflows, not extra steps that always happen together.    README.md starter  Copy code    # Company prototype\nSchool ICT project by [partner names or school-approved identifiers].\nUnofficial concept; not an endorsed company website.\n\nOpen index.html in a browser.\nPages: Home, About, Services, Contact.\nInteractions: [feature 1], [feature 2].\nAssets: [creator, source, licence/permission, edits].\nTesting: [devices/widths, actions, fixes].\nContributions and reflection: [each partner&#x27;s work].   "}, {"title": "Collaborate without overwriting each other", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#collaborate", "terms": "  Repository owner: open Settings \u2192 Collaborators (sometimes shown under access settings), add your partner\u2019s verified GitHub username, and send an invitation. The partner accepts it.  Agree on a small task for each partner. Both must program and understand the whole site. Avoid editing the same lines at the same time.  Optional GitHub Desktop workflow: File \u2192 Clone repository, choose the shared project, then open that local folder in your editor. GitHub Desktop is an optional Windows/macOS tool; Linux users can use the browser or a teacher-approved Git client.  Before editing, fetch and pull the latest changes. Save your own uncommitted work deliberately before switching tasks or integrating updates.  Edit and test locally. In the Changes view, inspect the diff, select the intended files, enter a specific summary, and commit to the current branch.  Push origin so your partner receives the commit. Your partner fetches/pulls before their next edit.  Review the History view together. Use meaningful commits such as \u201cFix mobile menu keyboard behavior\u201d, not \u201cstuff\u201d.   A commit count alone does not measure contribution. Discuss decisions, test each other\u2019s changes and document individual understanding.  Branches and a first merge conflict  For separate work, create a branch, commit and push it, then open a pull request on GitHub. Your partner reviews the changes before merging. If Git cannot combine edits to the same area, it reports a conflict. It does not know which version you intended.   Read both versions with your partner. Do not simply delete one person\u2019s work.  Use the co"}, {"title": "Optional publication: GitHub Pages", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#pages", "terms": "  Stop before making anything public: ask your teacher.  Public publication is optional. Ordinary GitHub Free personal repositories generally must be public to use Pages. Do not assume a private repository makes its Pages site private.   Obtain teacher approval. Check permissions, credits, identifying information and the unofficial-prototype label.  Prepare the repository: for main / (root), index.html must be at the repository root, next to your stylesheet and script, with images in their relative folder.  Open repository Settings \u2192 Pages.  Under Build and deployment \u2192 Source, select Deploy from a branch.  Choose main and / (root). Alternatively, use /docs only if index.html and all site files are inside that folder. Basic branch publishing allows root or /docs, not an arbitrary folder.  Save the settings. Wait for the deployment to finish; inspect the repository\u2019s Actions tab if it fails.  Open the site URL displayed in Pages settings. A project site normally uses https://USERNAME.github.io/REPOSITORY/. The repository\u2019s github.com URL is not the website URL.  Click every link and test your CSS, images and JavaScript on the hosted site.  To update it, commit and push revised files to the selected source. Deployment and browser caches can delay what you see.    Official publishing-source instructions  \u00b7  Create a Pages site   Pages serves static HTML, CSS and JavaScript. It does not run a custom PHP backend or server-side database. A classroom demonstration creates no obligation to host a company website permanently. Any official company use needs separate arrangements and "}, {"title": "When the hosted site looks wrong", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#troubleshooting", "terms": "     Symptom  Check      404 / file not found  Is index.html in the configured root or docs folder? Is it accidentally inside one extra folder?    Wrong page or repository listing  Use the published Pages URL, not the GitHub repository URL.    Missing CSS, JS or images  Check relative paths, filename extensions and exact capitalization. Coffee.svg and coffee.svg may differ.    No deployment  Check Pages source, branch, folder and Actions result. Make sure the branch exists and contains your files.    Old version  Check that your commit was pushed and deployment finished. Reload without cache after that.    Only subpages fail  Check every link relative to that page. Do not use absolute local disk paths or root-leading / paths in this portable project.      For this handbook, upload the  contents  of the extracted webdev-lab folder so index.html is at the repository root. Do not upload only the ZIP. "}, {"title": "What Is a Content Management System?", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#cms", "terms": " A CMS lets users create, edit and organize website content through an interface. Companies use it when staff need to update pages without editing HTML files directly. WordPress is one example.      Hand-coded static website  CMS-managed website      You edit HTML, CSS and JavaScript files.  Editors typically use forms or a visual editor in a dashboard.    Content is stored in files for this project.  Content is often stored in a database or another managed content store.    Shared design lives in your stylesheet.  Themes or templates control shared presentation.    Repository access governs who can change source.  Roles such as administrator, editor and author control permitted actions.    A static host can serve the finished files.  Hosting needs depend on the CMS; some CMS workflows export static files.    Updates require editing and publishing your files.  Authorized staff can draft, review and publish through the CMS.      A dashboard is the management area for pages, media, settings and users. Give people only the permissions they need. This project still uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript. This introduction does not replace practical CMS teaching and does not require installing WordPress.   Try it yourself  A caf\u00e9 owner wants to change the menu every day. Discuss when a CMS might help and what training or maintenance it would require.  "}, {"title": "Sources & learning scope", "page": "GitHub: build together.", "url": "github.html#sources", "terms": " This resource supports the European Schools ICT Complementary Course S6\u2013S7, document 2019-05-D-24-en-2, especially interactive web design, JavaScript, publication awareness, multimedia, independent project work and formative feedback. It is not coverage of the whole syllabus. CMS practice and other syllabus topics need additional teaching.    European Schools: ICT Complementary Course S6\u2013S7 syllabus    MDN: learn web development    MDN: HTML reference    MDN: CSS reference    MDN: JavaScript reference    GitHub Pages documentation    W3C: accessibility tutorials    All included coffee, pastry and mountain illustrations are original SVG graphics created for this resource. They are included locally and may be reused in this classroom project. Essential features do not depend on external links, services or libraries. "}, {"title": "Example: A Complete Company Website", "page": "A whole site. All the parts.", "url": "example.html#explore", "terms": "  Alpine Coffee is a fictional caf\u00e9 in Innsbruck.  Explore all four pages, try the menu filter and calculator, and open the FAQ. It is a learning reference, not a project to copy and submit unchanged.  Explore Alpine Coffee \u2197     A little taste of Innsbruck.    Four pages. One visual identity.  HTML structure, shared CSS and small, understandable interactions.   "}, {"title": "Follow the files", "page": "A whole site. All the parts.", "url": "example.html#files", "terms": "   Folder structure  Copy code    alpine-coffee/\n  index.html       Home and introduction\n  about.html       Story and concept\n  services.html    Menu filter and price calculator\n  contact.html     Example hours and FAQ\n  style.css        Shared design and mobile layout\n  script.js        Shared interaction handlers\n  images/\n    coffee.svg\n    mountains.svg\n    pastry.svg    Each page links to the other three by filename. All four load the same style.css and script.js. Every image path starts with images/ because the images folder sits next to these HTML pages. Copy the complete alpine-coffee folder to keep it standalone; the \u201cBack to handbook\u201d link needs the handbook alongside it. "}, {"title": "See how the parts connect", "page": "A whole site. All the parts.", "url": "example.html#connections", "terms": "   HTML file connections  Copy code    &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;style.css&quot;&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;script.js&quot; defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;!-- In the body --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;services.html&quot;&gt;Find your favorite&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;images/coffee.svg&quot; alt=&quot;A steaming cup of coffee&quot;&gt;    The CSS uses Grid for product cards and a media query at 700px to switch to one column. It also changes the navigation into a button-controlled menu. Images scale within their containers.  The JavaScript checks whether each page-specific control exists before attaching its listener. This lets the same script run on all four pages without trying to use missing elements. The filter compares data-category values; the calculator validates quantity before multiplication; FAQ buttons update hidden and aria-expanded. "}, {"title": "Inspect the complete source", "page": "A whole site. All the parts.", "url": "example.html#inspect", "terms": " Open developer tools to inspect the current DOM and CSS. To study the original files, use View Page Source or open the files in a text editor after extracting the project ZIP. The panels below include the complete shared CSS and JavaScript.   Complete style.css    alpine-coffee/style.css  Copy code    *{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;font:17px/1.7 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#293e34;background:#f8f3e9}a{color:inherit;text-underline-offset:4px}header{padding:25px 7%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;border-bottom:1px solid #d5d9cc;background:#f8f3e9}.logo{font:700 26px Georgia,serif;text-decoration:none}nav{display:flex;gap:24px}nav a{font-size:14px;text-decoration:none}nav a[aria-current]{text-decoration:underline}main{max-width:1180px;margin:auto;padding:40px 7% 65px}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-weight:400;line-height:1.1}h1{font-size:clamp(44px,7vw,78px);letter-spacing:-2px;margin:18px 0 24px}h2{font-size:36px}h3{font-size:25px}p{max-width:650px}.hero{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:40px;align-items:center;min-height:430px}.hero img{width:100%;border-radius:140px 140px 8px 8px}.eyebrow{font:12px system-ui;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase}.btn,button{display:inline-block;border:1px solid #304c3f;background:#304c3f;color:white;padding:12px 20px;border-radius:5px;text-decoration:none;font:inherit;cursor:pointer}button:hover,.btn:hover{background:#476b58}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:22px;margin-top:32px}.card{border:1px solid #d4d4c7;padding:23px;border-radius:9px;background"}, {"title": "Learn from the pattern", "page": "A whole site. All the parts.", "url": "example.html#adapt", "terms": "  Identify the purpose of each page before choosing a visual design.  Create your own business content and multimedia.  Use a feature only when it helps your audience.  Document sources, test small screens and explain your code.   The fictional company has no real contact details, form submission, payments or booking system. Those are deliberate prototype boundaries. "}];
